            "paragraph": "The Mellor-Yamada-Janjic (MYJ; Mellor and Yamada, 1982) planetary boundary layer (PBL) scheme and the shallow cumulus schemes (Hong and Jang, 2018) are utilized for the outer domain (d01 and d02) only.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model version 4.4.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "two-moment Morrison microphysics scheme",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Simulations in this study employ a two-moment Morrison microphysics scheme, which has been widely implemented in both CPMs and GCMs (Morrison et al., 2005; Morrison and Gettelman, 2008; Golaz et al., 2022).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model version 4.4.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "parameterization of Abdul-Razzak and Ghan (2000)",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Aerosol activation follows the parameterization of Abdul-Razzak and Ghan (2000), with fixed assumptions for size distribution, chemical composition, aerosol type, and mixing state.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model version 4.4.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "prescribed aerosol number concentration",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "To access the cloud responses to aerosol perturbations, we conduct three sets of simulations with different prescribed aerosol number concentration of N = 100, 500, and 1000 cm 3 for all 11 cases.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model version 4.4.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "LWP susceptibility",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "More specifically, LWP susceptibility in WRF simulations is defined as the change in domain mean cloud properties as dln(LWP)/dln(Na) between polluted and clean simulations for each 10 min model output.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model version 4.4.2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "precipitation fraction",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The modeled pixel-level precipitation fraction is calculated as the area fraction of cloudy pixels with the column maximum radar reflectivity (Zmax) greater than -15 dBZ at each model output time (Haynes et al., 2009; Suzuki et al., 2015; Jing et al., 2017).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        }
